RESEARCH_FINDING {
  department: "cybernetics",
  question: "How can Demerzel measure its variety ratio quantitatively?",
  conclusion: "Variety must be counted on outcomes, dimension by dimension (behavioral, structural, regulatory), with V = log2(N). Three counting rules apply: a product of component counts is a joint state space only if the components vary independently, and otherwise an upper bound; a count of rules is not a count of states, so an attenuator is measured by A = log2(N_in / N_out) on the outcomes that reach it and pass it; and an inventory is not a set of outcomes. No variety is measured yet. The inventory at commit 74cf7c5 and the definitions bound two of them from above: persona selection at most 3.81 bits (14 personas), and the decision labels, three separate vocabularies, at most 6.91 bits combined (120 combinations); how much of either is in use needs records of the personas chosen and the labels decisions carry. Structural, response and disturbance varieties and every attenuation are not measured; the disturbance estimates count sources, states and events rather than distinct disturbances, so they bound disturbance variety neither from below nor from above, and no Ashby gap can be computed from the inventory. Ashby's Law is stated on outcomes: V_D - V_R_amp bounds outcome variety only where no response brings two disturbances to the same outcome, so regulation is measured by recording, per cycle, each disturbance met, the response given and its outcome on the essential variables.",
  evidence: [
    "Ashby (1956) Chapter 7: V = log2(N) is the formal definition of variety",
    "Beer (1979) Chapter 6: variety engineering requires separate amplifier/attenuator accounting",
